{"v":15,"id":"e07","kid":"emma","title":"Marie Curie","zh":"玛丽·居里","level":"B1","genre":"人物传记","src":"facts","sum":"居里夫人出生在波兰，在巴黎求学，与丈夫皮埃尔发现钋和镭，是第一位获得诺贝尔奖的女性，也是唯一在两个不同科学领域获诺贝尔奖的人。","words":296,"paras":[{"t":"Marie Curie was one of the greatest scientists in history. She was born Maria Sklodowska in Warsaw, Poland, in 1867. Her parents were both teachers, and Maria loved learning from a young age. At that time, however, women in Poland were not allowed to go to university.","w":[[0,5,"Marie",-1],[6,11,"Curie",-1],[12,15,"was",-1],[16,19,"one",-1],[20,22,"of",-1],[23,26,"the",-1],[27,35,"greatest",-1],[36,46,"scientists",-1],[47,49,"in",-1],[50,57,"history",-1],[59,62,"she",-1],[63,66,"was",-1],[67,71,"born",-1],[72,77,"Maria",-1],[78,88,"Sklodowska",-1],[89,91,"in",-1],[92,98,"Warsaw",-1],[100,106,"poland",-1],[108,110,"in",-1],[117,120,"her",-1],[121,128,"parents",-1],[129,133,"were",-1],[134,138,"both",-1],[139,147,"teachers",-1],[149,152,"and",-1],[153,158,"Maria",-1],[159,164,"loved",-1],[165,173,"learning",-1],[174,178,"from",2],[179,180,"a",2],[181,186,"young",2],[187,190,"age",2],[192,194,"at",-1],[195,199,"that",-1],[200,204,"time",-1],[206,213,"however",-1],[215,220,"women",-1],[221,223,"in",-1],[224,230,"poland",-1],[231,235,"were",1],[236,239,"not",1],[240,247,"allowed",1],[248,250,"to",1],[251,253,"go",-1],[254,256,"to",-1],[257,267,"university",-1]]},{"t":"Maria did not give up. She worked as a governess, a teacher who lives with a family, and saved money for several years. In 1891, she moved to Paris, France, to study at the University of Paris. Life was hard. She lived in a small, cold room and sometimes did not have enough to eat. But she studied so hard that she finished first in her class in physics.","w":[[0,5,"Maria",-1],[6,9,"did",-1],[10,13,"not",-1],[14,18,"give",0],[19,21,"up",0],[23,26,"she",-1],[27,33,"worked",-1],[34,36,"as",-1],[37,38,"a",-1],[39,48,"governess",-1],[50,51,"a",-1],[52,59,"teacher",-1],[60,63,"who",-1],[64,69,"lives",-1],[70,74,"with",-1],[75,76,"a",-1],[77,83,"family",-1],[85,88,"and",-1],[89,94,"saved",-1],[95,100,"money",-1],[101,104,"for",-1],[105,112,"several",-1],[113,118,"years",-1],[120,122,"in",-1],[129,132,"she",-1],[133,138,"moved",-1],[139,141,"to",-1],[142,147,"paris",-1],[149,155,"france",-1],[157,159,"to",-1],[160,165,"study",-1],[166,168,"at",-1],[169,172,"the",-1],[173,183,"university",-1],[184,186,"of",-1],[187,192,"paris",-1],[194,198,"life",-1],[199,202,"was",-1],[203,207,"hard",-1],[209,212,"she",-1],[213,218,"lived",-1],[219,221,"in",-1],[222,223,"a",-1],[224,229,"small",-1],[231,235,"cold",-1],[236,240,"room",-1],[241,244,"and",-1],[245,254,"sometimes",-1],[255,258,"did",-1],[259,262,"not",-1],[263,267,"have",-1],[268,274,"enough",-1],[275,277,"to",-1],[278,281,"eat",-1],[283,286,"but",-1],[287,290,"she",-1],[291,298,"studied",-1],[299,301,"so",-1],[302,306,"hard",-1],[307,311,"that",-1],[312,315,"she",-1],[316,324,"finished",3],[325,330,"first",3],[331,333,"in",-1],[334,337,"her",-1],[338,343,"class",-1],[344,346,"in",-1],[347,354,"physics",-1]]},{"t":"In Paris she met Pierre Curie, a French scientist. They married in 1895 and worked together in a simple lab. They studied a new kind of energy that came from certain materials, and Marie named it \"radioactivity.\" In 1898, the couple discovered two new elements. Marie named one of them polonium, after her home country, Poland. The other was radium, which glowed in the dark.","w":[[0,2,"in",-1],[3,8,"paris",-1],[9,12,"she",-1],[13,16,"met",-1],[17,23,"Pierre",-1],[24,29,"Curie",-1],[31,32,"a",-1],[33,39,"french",-1],[40,49,"scientist",-1],[51,55,"they",-1],[56,63,"married",-1],[64,66,"in",-1],[72,75,"and",-1],[76,82,"worked",-1],[83,91,"together",-1],[92,94,"in",-1],[95,96,"a",-1],[97,103,"simple",-1],[104,107,"lab",-1],[109,113,"they",-1],[114,121,"studied",-1],[122,123,"a",-1],[124,127,"new",-1],[128,132,"kind",-1],[133,135,"of",-1],[136,142,"energy",-1],[143,147,"that",-1],[148,152,"came",-1],[153,157,"from",-1],[158,165,"certain",-1],[166,175,"materials",-1],[177,180,"and",-1],[181,186,"Marie",-1],[187,192,"named",-1],[193,195,"it",-1],[197,210,"radioactivity",-1],[213,215,"in",-1],[222,225,"the",-1],[226,232,"couple",-1],[233,243,"discovered",-1],[244,247,"two",-1],[248,251,"new",-1],[252,260,"elements",-1],[262,267,"Marie",-1],[268,273,"named",-1],[274,277,"one",-1],[278,280,"of",-1],[281,285,"them",-1],[286,294,"polonium",-1],[296,301,"after",-1],[302,305,"her",-1],[306,310,"home",10],[311,318,"country",10],[320,326,"poland",-1],[328,331,"the",-1],[332,337,"other",-1],[338,341,"was",-1],[342,348,"radium",-1],[350,355,"which",-1],[356,362,"glowed",-1],[363,365,"in",11],[366,369,"the",11],[370,374,"dark",11]]},{"t":"In 1903, Marie and Pierre shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with another scientist, Henri Becquerel. Marie was the first woman ever to win a Nobel Prize. Sadly, Pierre was killed in a street accident in 1906. Marie continued their work alone and became the first woman professor at the University of Paris. In 1911, she won a second Nobel Prize, this time in Chemistry. She is still the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.","w":[[0,2,"in",-1],[9,14,"Marie",-1],[15,18,"and",-1],[19,25,"Pierre",-1],[26,32,"shared",-1],[33,36,"the",-1],[37,42,"Nobel",4],[43,48,"prize",4],[49,51,"in",-1],[52,59,"physics",-1],[60,64,"with",-1],[65,72,"another",-1],[73,82,"scientist",-1],[84,89,"Henri",-1],[90,99,"Becquerel",-1],[101,106,"Marie",-1],[107,110,"was",-1],[111,114,"the",-1],[115,120,"first",-1],[121,126,"woman",-1],[127,131,"ever",-1],[132,134,"to",-1],[135,138,"win",-1],[139,140,"a",-1],[141,146,"Nobel",4],[147,152,"prize",4],[154,159,"sadly",-1],[161,167,"Pierre",-1],[168,171,"was",-1],[172,178,"killed",-1],[179,181,"in",-1],[182,183,"a",-1],[184,190,"street",-1],[191,199,"accident",-1],[200,202,"in",-1],[209,214,"Marie",-1],[215,224,"continued",-1],[225,230,"their",-1],[231,235,"work",-1],[236,241,"alone",-1],[242,245,"and",-1],[246,252,"became",